  "text": "We measured how leaf water loss changes over the day in two common garden plants using a standard moisture‑absorbing paper. For each plant we calculated a \"day‑night ratio\" by dividing the highest daytime water‑loss value by the low nighttime value; this ratio tells how much the rate varies each 24‑hour period. As a plant begins to wilt, the ratio moves toward 1, meaning day and night rates become similar, and often falls below 1 later because night loss exceeds day loss. The overall water‑loss index steadily drops during wilting until it reaches its lowest point at the permanent wilting stage; after that it may rise a little before the plant enters a final drying phase with very little loss. This pattern also appears in plants that have been removed from the soil. The minimum index is almost the same for a given species, whether the plant is rooted or cut, though soil type can cause some variation. Three moderate‑water‑need species showed similar critical values, suggesting each species has its own characteristic threshold. The amount of soil moisture left at this critical point also varies with plant type. Both the leaf water‑loss index and the remaining soil moisture at the critical stage can be used to compare drought tolerance, with lower numbers indicating greater resistance."
